Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Janbai (Devayani) and Shriji Maharaj In the epic history of the Mahabharata, the name of Devayani is renowned. She was the brilliant daughter of the sage Shukracharya, the preceptor of the Asuras. Yearning for spiritual liberation across countless ages, that elevated soul took birth in the remote village of Chhapari in Gohilwad. Her father&rsquo;s name was Ravat Chaitro and her mother was Kamalbai. Born as their daughter, this blessed soul was named Janbai.
Due to the continuous visits of holy sadhus, Ravat Chaitro&rsquo;s household was deeply immersed in the colors of Satsang. Ravat Chaitro frequently traveled to Gadhpur to have darshan of Maharaj, offer devotional service, and engage in the fellowship of holy sadhus.
Once, he brought his young daughter Janbai along for darshan. Amidst all the devotees, Janbai bowed reverently before Maharaj. For a long moment, Maharaj gazed intently at the young girl. Then He proclaimed, &ldquo;Behold! This is Devayani—the luminous daughter of Shukracharya! For thousands of years she has longed to meet Us! Today our reunion has taken place!&rdquo; Shriji Maharaj&rsquo;s nectarine words penetrated deep into Janbai&rsquo;s soul. Pierced by the arrow of divine love and devotion, her inner spiritual consciousness awakened, revealing the memories of her past lives. Every fiber of her being thrilled with supreme joy, and by Maharaj&rsquo;s sacred touch, her heart became entirely liberated from all worldly desires (nirvasanik).
Janbai returned home, but remained continuously absorbed in divine worship. As she grew older, her parents began searching for a suitable groom. Learning of this, Janbai pleaded with her mother, &ldquo;Mother! Please do not trouble yourself searching for a groom. I do not wish to lead a worldly life; I wish to become a sankhyayogi (renunciant devotee).&rdquo; Her parents urged her to marry, but Janbai remained steadfast. Witnessing their daughter&rsquo;s intense yearning for God, the parents happily consented, saying, &ldquo;As you wish, daughter. As parents, our instinct was to settle you in household life; but since you wish to devote your life to God, we shall not stand in your way. A virtuous daughter like you will liberate seventy-one generations of our lineage!&rdquo;
Thus, her parents paved the way for her to practice the life of a renunciant while living at home. One day, Janbai requested, &ldquo;Mother, I require a separate room to worship Shriji Maharaj; living amongst everyone causes distraction in my prayers and meditation.&rdquo; Her parents gladly provided her an independent chamber. There, Janbai engaged in uninterrupted meditation on Maharaj. She sat in one posture through the entire night meditating on Shri Hari. To master her posture, she placed a heavy grinding stone atop her knees; to conquer sleep and lethargy, she tied her hair braid with a rope suspended from the ceiling beam, and severely curbed her physical senses by consuming minimal nourishment. In this manner, she embarked upon rigorous spiritual penance to please the Lord.
Once, her mother Kamalbai was preparing to visit Gadhpur and urged Janbai to accompany her. When Janbai declined, her mother gently chided her, &ldquo;Foolish girl! Day and night you chant Maharaj&rsquo;s name, you meditate on Him constantly, yet you refuse to come for His darshan?&rdquo; Janbai replied with loving obstinacy (prem-hath), &ldquo;Mother, if Maharaj has need of me, He will personally come here to grant darshan; I shall not go.&rdquo; These words harbored no trace of arrogance; they were born of deep, unalloyed love. When Kamalbai arrived in Gadhpur, Maharaj inquired, &ldquo;Why did Our Janbai not come?&rdquo; Kamalbai replied, &ldquo;Maharaj! That stubborn girl insisted on staying home, saying that if Maharaj needs her, He will come Himself.&rdquo; Hearing this, Maharaj smiled gently and said, &ldquo;Indeed, Janu is Devayani herself! Being Shukracharya&rsquo;s daughter, some trace of pride is natural!&rdquo;
Sometime later, when Dada Khachar&rsquo;s wedding procession departed from Gadhpur, Shriji Maharaj Himself took the reins as the charioteer of the groom&rsquo;s carriage, accompanied by an assembly of sadhus. Shri Hari arrived with the wedding party at Bhattavadar, and simultaneously manifested in His divine form in the courtyard of Ravat Chaitro in Chhapari village!
Beholding Maharaj standing in their courtyard, Ravat Chaitro, Jutho Chaitro, and Kamalbai were overwhelmed with ecstatic devotion. Kamalbai lovingly laid out a cot, upon which Maharaj sat. Ravat and Kamalbai washed Maharaj&rsquo;s holy feet and sanctified themselves with charnamrit. Maharaj asked, &ldquo;Where is Our devotee Janbai?&rdquo; Kamalbai replied, &ldquo;Maharaj, she never steps outside her room; she is continuously absorbed in meditating upon You.&rdquo; Maharaj smiled and asked playfully, &ldquo;Will she come out now, or must We become even more desperate and walk into her room?&rdquo;
&ldquo;I shall call her right away,&rdquo; Kamalbai rushed and called out, &ldquo;Janu, dear daughter! Come out at once, Maharaj Himself has arrived!&rdquo; Hearing these words, Janbai rushed out and prostrated at Maharaj&rsquo;s lotus feet. As she beheld His divine form, streams of tears flowed from her eyes. With a choked voice she said, &ldquo;O Krupanath! At last You have shown boundless mercy upon this servant!&rdquo; Maharaj laughed and said, &ldquo;O foolish one! Through your persistent obstinacy, you made Us yearn for you so deeply!&rdquo; Janbai replied with a smile, &ldquo;After all, Lord, I am Shukracharya&rsquo;s daughter; bodies change across births, but innate nature takes time to transform!&rdquo; Maharaj replied, &ldquo;No, Janbai! Your nature was purified long ago through the glory of meditating upon Us.&rdquo;
Janbai prayed, &ldquo;Maharaj, forgive me for troubling You unnecessarily with my stubbornness. But Lord, had You not graced us with Your presence, how could this home, this darbargadh, and this entire village be sanctified?&rdquo; Maharaj responded, &ldquo;Janbai, We know the purity of your heart, and that is why We have come. Because of this connection with Us, whoever takes birth in your family—even anyone who visits as a guest—all shall attain supreme liberation!&rdquo;
Hearing Maharaj&rsquo;s blessings, Janbai pleaded with tears in her eyes, &ldquo;Lord, that is Your infinite grace; but how much longer must I wander in this mundane world? I have been wandering across countless epochs. When will my deliverance come? Forgive the shortcomings of this humble soul and grant me liberation now.&rdquo;
Maharaj gave His divine promise: &ldquo;Janu, you will wander no longer. Exactly one month from today, We shall come to take you to Akshardham.&rdquo;
Janbai added, &ldquo;Maharaj, I am not alone; we are three companions.&rdquo; Maharaj inquired, &ldquo;Who are the other two?&rdquo; Janbai replied, &ldquo;One is myself, the second is my cousin, and the third is the wife of the Vania who became an earthbound spirit (chudel). We three have pledged to enter Your divine abode together.&rdquo; Maharaj chuckled, &ldquo;Do you wish to bring that ghost to Our divine abode as well?&rdquo; Janbai said, &ldquo;Lord, I have enlightened her about Your supreme glory; she is a deserving soul. Please extend Your grace upon her.&rdquo; Maharaj granted His assurance: &ldquo;As you desire. After one month, We shall arrive to take all three of you together.&rdquo;
Hearing this extraordinary dialogue, the entire family was awestruck. Exactly one month later, Shriji Maharaj arrived with a celestial chariot (viman) to take Janbai. Janbai had already informed everyone in advance: &ldquo;Maharaj has arrived; we three are boarding the celestial chariot to proceed to Maharaj&rsquo;s divine abode. All of you, take firm refuge in Pragat Purushottam Narayan and continue chanting His holy name. Jai Swaminarayan to all!&rdquo;
Divine muktas showered fragrant flowers from the heavens. Shedding her mortal frame, Janbai assumed a radiant, divine body and departed with Shri Hari for the eternal abode of Akshardham.
