Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Mengani Darbar Family and Their Steadfast Satsang Nishtha Sadguru Gopalanand Swami frequently visited the royal court (darbar) of Darbar Mansinh Bapu (fondly known as Manbha Bapu) of Mengani. It was through association with Swami that Darbar Manbha Bapu renounced all addictions, including alcohol and meat, and wholeheartedly embraced Satsang. Blessed by Gopalanand Swami, he became widely celebrated across the Satsang fellowship as an Ekantik devotee—&ldquo;Manbha Bapu of Mengani.&rdquo; Manbha Bapu&rsquo;s son was Amarsinhji, to whom Shri Hari granted divine darshan in his youth and took him directly to Akshardham. By the blessings of Gopalanand Swami, Amarsinhji had married at a young age; before departing to the divine abode, he was blessed with two sons: Madhavsinhji and Merumbha Bapu. Merumbha Bapu subsequently had five sons, one of whom was named Bhimsinhji.
Once, Sadguru Balamukunddas Swami, a beloved and spiritually elevated disciple of Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami, visited Mengani along with his disciple Sadguru Madhavprasad Swami (the guru of Sadguru Mohanprasad Swami of Dhoraji) and stayed at the village mandir. Learning of the arrival of the revered santos, young Kunwar Bhimsinhji ran enthusiastically to the mandir for their darshan. Entering the sanctum, he offered prostrations (dandvat pranam) before Thakorji and then reverently prostrated at the feet of Sadguru Balamukunddas Swami. Swami, possessed of immense spiritual prowess and whose words unfailingly came to pass (vachan-siddha), gently placed his hand upon young Kunwar Bhimsinhji. Gazing deeply into his countenance and discerning his impending destiny upon his forehead, Swami asked, &ldquo;Darbar! Do you wish to become a sadhu?&rdquo;
Bhimsinhji, an exalted soul (yogabhrashta atma) carried over from past spiritual pursuits, instantly replied, &ldquo;Yes, Gurudev! I want to renounce and become a sadhu.&rdquo;
Swami smiled and said, &ldquo;Then come along with us! But to become a sadhu, first obtain your father&rsquo;s permission.&rdquo;
The young Bhimsinhji rushed straight to his father and pleaded, &ldquo;Father, please grant me permission. I wish to become a sadhu and serve eternally at the holy feet of Gurudev Balamukunddas Swami.&rdquo;
Hearing this, Darbar Kalyansinhji—though a devoted soul who harbored immense love for the santos—hesitated to let his young son renounce the world. Gripped by parental attachment (putramoha), he gently deferred the request, saying, &ldquo;Son, you are still too young; grow up first, and then you may become a sadhu.&rdquo;
Disheartened, Bhimsinhji returned to the mandir in the evening and confided, &ldquo;Swami, my father will not grant permission.&rdquo;
Hearing his reply, Swami said with quiet surrender, &ldquo;As Maharaj wills!&rdquo; (Jevi Maharaj ni marji!). After spending a few days imparting spiritual bliss through discourses at Mengani, Sadguru Balamukunddas Swami departed for other villages.
Shortly thereafter, Kunwar Bhimsinhji was stricken with a severe fever. The illness proved intractable, worsening like an incurable disease despite numerous medications, remedies, and dedicated treatments. The entire royal household tended to him day and night at his bedside, yet the young prince’s health continued to decline. One day, Bhimsinhji spoke with calm clarity: &ldquo;Father, Shriji Maharaj Himself has arrived to escort me to His abode; and Gurudev Balamukunddas Swami and Madhavprasad Swami have also arrived with Him.&rdquo;
Assuming that the youth was speaking in a feverish delirium, the family did not grasp the divine gravity of his words. Moments later, Kunwar Bhimsinhji greeted everyone with a final &ldquo;Jai Swaminarayan&rdquo; and peacefully departed this mortal world for Akshardham. The entire Darbargadh was plunged into profound grief at the untimely passing of the young prince. With heavy hearts, the family performed his funeral rites.
Some time later, Sadguru Balamukunddas Swami returned to Mengani during his spiritual travels. Darbar Kalyansinhji went to pay his respects and broke down in tears before Swami, lamenting the loss of his son. Swami consoled him with deep compassion and unveiled the hidden truth:
&ldquo;Darbar, when Kunwar Bhimsinhji came for our darshan at the mandir, we read the inscription written upon his forehead. We distinctly perceived the approaching shadow of Kala (death) hovering over him. It was solely to ward off that untimely demise that we asked him to become a sadhu. Had he renounced and taken the path of sainthood, Shriji Maharaj would have extended his lifespan (aavarda). But bound by maternal and paternal attachment, you could not perceive the profound purpose behind our words. Nevertheless, whatever Shri Hari wills is supreme. Your son has attained the divine abode of Shri Hari, basking in the eternal bliss of Shriji Maharaj. Therefore, grieve no more with worldly sorrow.&rdquo;
Hearing Swami’s reassuring words, Darbar Kalyansinhji found solace and inner peace. After conducting spiritual discourses for several days, Balamukunddas Swami and Madhavprasad Swami journeyed on toward Junagadh.
The royal lineage of Manbha Bapu of Mengani, blessed to be spiritual disciples of Sadguru Gopalanand Swami, produced many noble and liberated souls. In this very lineage was born Namdar Darbar Arjunsinhji, who later served as the Collector of Vadodara. Once, while walking down a public street, he noticed Gurudev Shastriji Shri Dharmajivandasji Swami passing by in a vehicle. Setting aside all bureaucratic status, royal prestige, and pride, he immediately fell prostrate in full dandvat pranam before Swami right in the bustling bazaar—such was his sublime egoless humility (nirmanipanu) before holy sadhus!
Another distinguished scion was Kumar Pradyumnsinhji, who was educated in England and became an IAS officer, ultimately serving with great distinction as the Chief Secretary of Gujarat State. In 1948, when Shree Swaminarayan Gurukul Rajkot was founded, the sacred ground-breaking ceremony (bhumi pujan) was performed by the hands of Kumar Pradyumnsinhji. Furthermore, the foundation-stone laying ceremony (khat-muhurta) of the Shree Swaminarayan Mandir on Bhupendra Road in Rajkot was also performed by him.
To this day, the noble Darbar family, recipients of Sadguru Gopalanand Swami’s eternal grace, preserves this steadfast devotion and deep-rooted Satsang tradition.
From the life accounts of Sadguru Shri Balamukunddas Swami&hellip; 🙏
