Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Miyaji of Ningala and Shriji Maharaj At the loving invitation of Queen Kushalkunwarba of Dharampur, Shri Hari departed from Gadhpur (Gadhada) for Dharampur. Journeying alongside an assembly of revered sants and devotees, He halted on the way at the village of Ningala near Botad to visit the humble residence of His devoted attendant, Miyaji. Shri Hari would often lovingly remark to all, &ldquo;The true masters and protectors of this Darbargarh are Ratanji and Miyaji!&rdquo;
Arriving in Ningala, Shri Hari called out Miyaji&rsquo;s name while standing in the street right outside the courtyard wall of his home. Hearing the divine voice of his Lord, Miyaji came rushing out and fell prostrate in humble dandvat pranam. Shri Hari immediately lifted him up and embraced him with profound affection.
Miyaji respectfully escorted everyone into his home. In the veranda (osari), he spread fresh traditional quilts (dhadki) and bolsters upon a cot (khatlo), seating Shriji Maharaj with supreme reverence. Miyaji served cool drinking water to everyone, applied an auspicious sandalwood mark (chandlo) upon Shri Hari&rsquo;s fair cheeks, and reverently worshiped Him by offering a coconut and flowers. Shri Hari affectionately inquired about Miyaji&rsquo;s wellbeing. Sitting right there on the cot, Shri Hari penned an extraordinary letter of spiritual glory (mahima-patra) written in a unique blend of half Hindi and half Gujarati:
&ldquo;On the auspicious day of Samvat 1872, Magshar Sud 1, Miyaji Ratanji hosted our sacred arrival (padhramani). In honor of this spiritual glory, a divine assembly took place comprising Shree Sahajanandji, Dada Khachar, sadhus, brahmacharis, and all our satsangi devotees. Devotional dhun was chanted. We have sanctified the premises by leaving our auspicious footprints (charan-pagla). Although Miyaji is a Muslim, all souls are equal in our eyes. We perceive no distinction between Hindu and Muslim; all living beings are equal unto us. Miyaji has truly recognized our divine identity; hence, unto our humble servant Miyaji, we have bestowed our sanctified (prasadi) garments, sacred foot-impressions, and divine gifts. Whosoever reads this declaration of divine glory or has its holy darshan, we shall personally escort that soul to Akshardham. And whoever has its darshan daily shall merge into our divine, eternal effulgence.
— Written by Shree Sahajanandji&rdquo;
Thus, by gracing Miyaji&rsquo;s home, Shri Hari embraced his pure, unalloyed love transcending all barriers of religion, and bestowed upon him the supreme promise of eternal redemption in Akshardham.
— From Bhagwan Shri Swaminarayan&hellip;
