Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Vanik Sheth of Sarvai and the Loving Service of Mulji Brahmachari Once, Shriji Maharaj was gracing the Darbargadh of the village chieftain Jivna Dhadhal in the village of Sarvai, near Lathidad. At that time, a devoted satsangi Vanik Sheth (merchant) arrived with his attendants carrying large trays filled with pure crystallized sugar (sakar) to offer to Shri Hari. Placing the trays of sakar near Shriji Maharaj’s cot (dholiyo), the merchant offered prostrations (dandvat pranam) and sat reverently before Him.
Seeing the merchant’s pure devotion and love, Shri Hari scooped up a handful of sugar from the tray and began relishing it. At that very moment, Shri Hari’s devoted personal attendant, Mulji Brahmachari, arrived to invite Shriji Maharaj to partake of the meal (thal) he had lovingly prepared. Seeing Maharaj eating sugar, Mulji Brahmachari became upset and spoke with affectionate annoyance: &ldquo;I have prepared an elaborate meal for You, and here You are eating sugar! Now the food I cooked will be left untouched and go to waste!&rdquo;
Shri Hari lovingly consoled him, saying, &ldquo;Do not worry; We will certainly eat your thal as well! Today, you are at fault, yet We hold no grievance against you; while We have done no wrong, yet you are displeased with Us without any fault of Ours.&rdquo;
Folding his hands in humility, Mulji Brahmachari replied, &ldquo;O Lord! Is it my fault that I stopped You from eating sugar? If I ever forbid You again in the future, dismiss me from Your personal service! Whatever we do through mind, word, and deed is solely to please You. If there is any other mistake on my part, please tell me!&rdquo;
When Shri Hari remained silent, Brahmachariji spoke further with devotional intimacy: &ldquo;You are One who never remains bound by anyone&rsquo;s attachment! Just as in Your earlier incarnation You renounced Sitaji over the idle words of a washerman, not hesitating for a moment to forego even Your divine title as the &lsquo;Ocean of Mercy&rsquo; (Karunasindhu)!&rdquo;
Hearing this, Shri Hari replied with profound grace: &ldquo;We forgive millions of mistakes made by Our devotees. Even if someone repeatedly perceives flaws in Us, We never abandon anyone who has taken refuge at Our feet. In the past as well, without dwelling on anyone&rsquo;s faults, We have bestowed divine fruits upon all; We never keep anyone&rsquo;s account pending.&rdquo;
Moved to tears by these words of supreme compassion, Mulji Brahmachari folded his hands and prayed: &ldquo;O Maharaj! Whenever we make a mistake, please correct us just like this and keep guiding and reforming us until our final breath.&rdquo;
Smiling divinely, Shri Hari arose and happily accompanied Mulji Brahmachari to partake of the sanctified thal.
From the chronicles of Brahmacharis, Worshippers of the Supreme Brahman&hellip; 🙏
