Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Groundbreaking of the Gadhpur Mandir and the Supreme Surrender of Dada Khachar Once in Gadhpur, Shri Hari was seated on a cot on the veranda of the north-facing room. At that time, Jiva Khachar came, bowed at Shri Hari&rsquo;s lotus feet, and asked, &ldquo;Maharaj! Yesterday You resolved to construct a mandir here; have You started gathering the necessary materials?&rdquo; Shri Hari replied, &ldquo;Jiva Bapu, We haven&rsquo;t started any of that yet.&rdquo;
When Jiva Bapu repeatedly inquired about this for two or three consecutive days, Shriji Maharaj called Dada Khachar, Laduba, and Jivuba, and said, &ldquo;Let us order iron and building materials from Bhavnagar and commence the mandir construction.&rdquo; Dada Khachar replied, &ldquo;O Maharaj! If You instruct Ladha Thakkar, he will make all the necessary arrangements to procure everything from Bhavnagar.&rdquo;
That day in the assembly, Shri Hari called Ladha Thakkar and said, &ldquo;Jiva Khachar has been insisting for the past few days, so let us begin the work of the mandir!&rdquo; Jiva Khachar reiterated, &ldquo;Maharaj, please start the mandir!&rdquo; and stood up to leave. In the assembly, Shri Hari remarked, &ldquo;Look at Jiva Khachar&rsquo;s earnest enthusiasm for building the mandir!&rdquo; Ladha Thakkar then advised, &ldquo;Maharaj, first get the written deed for the land from him! Jiva Khachar is like the turning of a cart&rsquo;s wheel—he can change his mind at any moment.&rdquo; Shri Hari replied, &ldquo;He is a reputable chieftain, just like Hemasha and Bhaga Doshi of Botad; he will not back out!&rdquo; But the prudent Ladha Thakkar cautioned, &ldquo;Maharaj, I do not trust him. First, have him execute a written legal deed in the presence of witnesses; only then should we order the materials from Bhavnagar.&rdquo; Shri Hari acknowledged, &ldquo;Ladha Thakkar, you speak with great wisdom. When Jiva Bapu comes tomorrow, keep paper and pen ready, and we shall prepare the deed then.&rdquo;
The next day, as Shri Hari was seated on His cot, Jiva Bapu arrived, bowed down, and inquired about ordering materials for the mandir. Shri Hari said, &ldquo;Ladha Thakkar has brought pen and paper, so let us first execute the land deed, and then begin ordering the supplies!&rdquo; Jiva Khachar hesitated and said, &ldquo;Maharaj, let it be for today; we will do it tomorrow!&rdquo; When the assembly dispersed, Ladha Thakkar told Maharaj, &ldquo;Now watch tomorrow what kind of deed Jiva Bapu signs for You!&rdquo; Shri Hari said, &ldquo;Since Jiva Bapu promised in the assembly, he will surely sign the deed!&rdquo;
The following day, when the assembly gathered again, Shri Hari reminded Jiva Bapu to sign the land deed for the mandir. Once again, Jiva Bapu evaded, &ldquo;Let it be for today; tomorrow we will do it in front of everyone.&rdquo; Shri Hari said, &ldquo;Since you agreed yesterday, We thought we would write the deed today.&rdquo; At this, Jiva Khachar bluntly revealed his hesitation, &ldquo;Maharaj! In times of conflict and battle (dhinganu), this Darbargarh serves as our stronghold! Please build the mandir somewhere else!&rdquo; With this pretext, he flatly refused to give land for the mandir. Shri Hari praised Ladha Thakkar&rsquo;s shrewd judgment, noting that he possessed a keen ability to read people. The assembly dispersed, everyone returned to their quarters, and Shri Hari retired to Akshar Ordi.
A short while later, Bhaga Doshi of Botad, Somla Khachar, Sura Khachar of Loya, Jiva Khachar of Sarangpur, and Vasta Khachar of Kariyani gathered at Akshar Ordi. They bowed at Shri Hari&rsquo;s lotus feet on the veranda and sat down.
Sura Khachar offered, &ldquo;Maharaj! Please come to Loya. I will give as much land as needed for the mandir and vacate my entire Darbargarh for the sadhus to reside.&rdquo; Vasta Khachar offered, &ldquo;Build it in Kariyani.&rdquo; Jiva Khachar of Sarangpur insisted, &ldquo;Build it in Sarangpur; our region is under Limbdi&rsquo;s rule, so there will be no disputes or political interference.&rdquo; While discussions were underway, Shri Hari suddenly announced, &ldquo;Come, yoke the bullock cart!&rdquo; Saying this, He climbed onto the cart and proceeded forward, reaching the spot where the shrine of Ganapati stands today.
Upon hearing this news, Laduba rushed frantically to Dada Khachar and said, &ldquo;Brother! Jiva Khachar is taking Shri Hari away to Sarangpur to build the mandir there! Go stop Him immediately so that we may have the mandir built right here!&rdquo;
Dada Khachar ran swiftly and prostrated flat on the road directly in front of the cart. Shri Hari asked the cart driver, &ldquo;Why have you stopped the cart?&rdquo; The driver replied, &ldquo;Dada Khachar is lying in the road.&rdquo; Shri Hari said, &ldquo;Steer the cart slightly to the side and proceed.&rdquo; But Dada Khachar immediately shifted and lay across the cart&rsquo;s path again. Shri Hari said, &ldquo;Today We are certainly going to Sarangpur and will build the mandir there alone.&rdquo;
Hearing this, Dada Khachar began to weep inconsolably, his heart overflowing with intense devotion. He ran forward, embraced Shri Hari tightly, and cried out through his tears, &ldquo;Maharaj! If it pleases You, build the mandir on top of this fort; I will contest legally before the government and register it in Your name! Build the mandir anywhere You wish throughout the Darbargarh; demolish my own residential rooms to construct the mandir! In this entire Darbargarh, I hold an equal half-share alongside my uncle. But please, stay here forever!&rdquo;
Witnessing such extraordinary devotion, Somla Khachar pleaded, &ldquo;O Maharaj! Please grant Dada Khachar&rsquo;s heartfelt prayer and remain here.&rdquo; Shri Hari turned the cart back. In Gadhpur itself, He had the south-facing rooms dismantled, selected an auspicious time (muhurt), and with His own divine hands, inaugurated the groundbreaking and construction of the glorious mandir.
